WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.740
How do you see one whole cell in a crowded brain?

2
00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:09.130
A Nissl stain marks every cell body.

3
00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:14.610
But axons and dendrites, where the question of fusion lives, stay almost invisible.

4
00:00:15.783 --> 00:00:19.823
In 1873, Camillo Golgi found the black reaction.

5
00:00:20.843 --> 00:00:21.843
Most cells stay pale.

6
00:00:22.663 --> 00:00:24.783
A few fill with black, to the finest twig.

7
00:00:25.717 --> 00:00:30.317
This is sparse labelling: a few cells, filled completely, apparently at random.

8
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.940
Picture a forest at night with a few trees lit.

9
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.240
Each lit tree's shape stands out.

10
00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:41.170
Each black branch can be traced back to its own cell, where the section allows.

11
00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:45.090
But a contact has two sides, and both are rarely stained.

12
00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:50.390
Even where two black branches meet, the gap is far below what light can show.

13
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:55.600
Where two lit branches cross in the dark, you still cannot tell whether they are

14
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:56.600
grafted together.

15
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.400
In 1887, Santiago Ramón y Cajal took up the stain.

16
00:01:02.433 --> 00:01:06.453
Golgi read the tangle of axon branches as a continuous net.

17
00:01:07.417 --> 00:01:12.117
Cajal found branches ending close to other cells without joining them, again and

18
00:01:12.117 --> 00:01:13.117
again.

19
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.180
Neither could win at the contact.

20
00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.260
So Cajal turned to embryos, where cells are fewer and simpler.

21
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:24.840
Wilhelm His argued that each fibre grows out of a single cell, a neuroblast.

22
00:01:25.883 --> 00:01:28.623
In 1890, Cajal named the growth cone.

23
00:01:29.523 --> 00:01:32.383
He read it as the advancing end of a growing fibre.

24
00:01:33.483 --> 00:01:36.243
Suppose every fibre does grow out of one cell.

25
00:01:37.103 --> 00:01:38.523
Does that rule out Golgi's net?

26
00:01:44.917 --> 00:01:49.297
No. Outgrowth shows where a fibre begins, not how it ends.

27
00:01:50.077 --> 00:01:52.677
A fibre could grow out, then fuse at its far end.

28
00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:57.470
So embryos made separate cells more likely, without settling it.

29
00:02:01.783 --> 00:02:03.123
Cajal added a rule.

30
00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:07.963
Signals enter through the dendrites and cell body, and leave through the axon.

31
00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:12.153
In a chain of separate cells, their order sets a direction.

32
00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:14.573
Continuity alone sets none.

33
00:02:15.433 --> 00:02:16.453
The rule has exceptions.

34
00:02:17.567 --> 00:02:21.047
In 1906, Golgi and Cajal shared the Nobel Prize.

35
00:02:22.147 --> 00:02:23.527
Golgi still defended his net.

36
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.580
No light microscope could show the gap.

37
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.900
Separate cells were the better explanation, but nobody had seen it.
